#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:5
msgid "The Linux NTFS filesystem driver"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:17
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:19
msgid ""
"NTFS is a Linux kernel filesystem driver that provides full read and write "
"support for NTFS volumes. It is designed for high performance, modern kernel "
"infrastructure (iomap, folio), and stable long-term maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:25
msgid "Utilities support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:27
msgid ""
"The NTFS utilities project, called ntfsprogs-plus, provides mkfs.ntfs, "
"fsck.ntfs, and other related tools (e.g., ntfsinfo, ntfsclone, etc.) for "
"creating, checking, and managing NTFS volumes. These utilities can be used "
"for filesystem testing with xfstests as well as for recovering corrupted "
"NTFS devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:33
msgid "The project is available at:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:35
msgid "https://github.com/ntfsprogs-plus/ntfsprogs-plus"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:39
msgid "Linux file attributes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:41
msgid ""
"The driver supports lsattr(1) and chattr(1). Immutable, append-only, nodump "
"and noatime are stored in a private ``$LINUXFLAGS`` extended attribute "
"within the standard NTFS ``$EA``/``$EA_INFORMATION`` attributes. Its value "
"is a 32-bit little-endian bitmask: bit 0 is immutable, bit 1 is append-only, "
"bit 2 is nodump, and bit 3 is noatime. Unknown bits are preserved when "
"updating the flags; the entry is removed when the entire value becomes zero. "
"These settings survive inode eviction, unmount and reboot. Direct writes to "
"this EA through setxattr(2) or removexattr(2) are rejected; use chattr(1) "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:50
msgid ""
"The noatime attribute (``chattr +A``) suppresses automatic access-time "
"updates for the inode, including when mounted with ``strictatime``. It "
"applies to directories as well as files, but is not inherited by newly "
"created children. Use ``chattr -A`` to restore the mount's access-time "
"policy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:55
msgid ""
"System metadata files and files protected by the ``sys_immutable`` mount "
"option cannot have their immutable protection cleared with chattr(1). "
"Protection derived solely from the mount option is not stored in the EA. "
"Case folding is reported according to the mount options and cannot be "
"configured per file. Compression and encryption are reported but cannot be "
"changed through chattr(1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:62
msgid ""
"These Linux flags are enforced by Linux. Storing them in an NTFS EA does not "
"make Windows enforce immutable, append-only, nodump or noatime semantics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:67
msgid "Supported mount options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:69
msgid "The NTFS driver supports the following mount options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:72
msgid "iocharset=name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:72
msgid ""
"Character set to use for converting between the encoding is used for user "
"visible filename and 16 bit Unicode characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:76
msgid "nls=name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:76
msgid ""
"Deprecated option.  Still supported but please use iocharset=name in the "
"future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:79
msgid "uid="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:80
msgid "gid="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:81
msgid "umask="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:81
msgid ""
"Provide default owner, group, and access mode mask. These options work as "
"documented in mount(8).  By default, the files/directories are owned by root "
"and he/she has read and write permissions, as well as browse permission for "
"directories.  No one else has any access permissions.  I.e. the mode on all "
"files is by default rw------- and for directories rwx------, a consequence "
"of the default fmask=0177 and dmask=0077. Using a umask of zero will grant "
"all permissions to everyone, i.e. all files and directories will have mode "
"rwxrwxrwx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:94
msgid "fmask="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:95
msgid "dmask="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:95
msgid ""
"Instead of specifying umask which applies both to files and directories, "
"fmask applies only to files and dmask only to directories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:99
msgid "showmeta=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:100
msgid "show_sys_files=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:100
msgid ""
"If show_sys_files is specified, show the system files in directory "
"listings.  Otherwise the default behaviour is to hide the system files. Note "
"that even when show_sys_files is specified, \"$MFT\" will not be visible due "
"to bugs/mis-features in glibc. Further, note that irrespective of "
"show_sys_files, all files are accessible by name, i.e. you can always do "
"\"ls -l \\$UpCase\" for example to specifically show the system file "
"containing the Unicode upcase table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:111
msgid "case_sensitive=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:111
msgid ""
"If case_sensitive is specified, treat all filenames as case sensitive and "
"create file names in the POSIX namespace (default behavior). Note, the Linux "
"NTFS driver will never create short filenames and will remove them on rename/"
"delete of the corresponding long file name. Note that files remain "
"accessible via their short file name, if it exists."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:120
msgid "nocase=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:120
msgid "If nocase is specified, treat filenames case-insensitively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:123
msgid "disable_sparse=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:123
msgid ""
"If disable_sparse is specified, creation of sparse regions, i.e. holes, "
"inside files is disabled for the volume (for the duration of this mount "
"only). By default, creation of sparse regions is enabled, which is "
"consistent with the behaviour of traditional Unix filesystems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:130
msgid "errors=opt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:130
msgid ""
"Specify NTFS behavior on critical errors: panic, remount the partition in "
"read-only mode or continue without doing anything (default behavior)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:134
msgid "mft_zone_multiplier="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:134
msgid ""
"Set the MFT zone multiplier for the volume (this setting is not persistent "
"across mounts and can be changed from mount to mount but cannot be changed "
"on remount).  Values of 1 to 4 are allowed, 1 being the default.  The MFT "
"zone multiplier determines how much space is reserved for the MFT on the "
"volume.  If all other space is used up, then the MFT zone will be shrunk "
"dynamically, so this has no impact on the amount of free space.  However, it "
"can have an impact on performance by affecting fragmentation of the MFT. In "
"general use the default.  If you have a lot of small files then use a higher "
"value.  The values have the following meaning:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:150
msgid "Value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:150
msgid "MFT zone size (% of volume size)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:152
msgid "1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:152
msgid "12.5%"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:153
msgid "2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:153
msgid "25%"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:154
msgid "3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:154
msgid "37.5%"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:155
msgid "4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:155
msgid "50%"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:158
msgid "Note this option is irrelevant for read-only mount."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:160
msgid "preallocated_size="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:160
msgid ""
"Set preallocated size to optimize runlist merge overhead with small chunck "
"size.(64KB size by default)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:164
msgid "acl=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:164
msgid ""
"Enable POSIX ACL support. When specified, POSIX ACLs stored in extended "
"attributes are enforced. Default is off. Requires kernel config "
"NTFS_FS_POSIX_ACL enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:169
msgid "sys_immutable=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:169
msgid ""
"Make NTFS system files (e.g. $MFT, $LogFile, $Bitmap, $UpCase, etc.) "
"immutable to user initiated modifications for extra safety. Default is off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:173
msgid "nohidden=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:173
msgid ""
"Hide files and directories marked with the Windows \"hidden\" attribute. By "
"default hidden items are shown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:177
msgid "hide_dot_files=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:177
msgid ""
"Hide names beginning with a dot (\".\"). By default dot files are shown. "
"When enabled, files and directories created with a leading '.' will be "
"hidden from directory listings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:182
msgid "windows_names=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:182
msgid ""
"Refuse creation/rename of files with characters or reserved device names "
"disallowed on Windows (e.g. CON, NUL, AUX, COM1, LPT1, etc.). Default is off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:185
msgid "discard=<BOOL>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:185
msgid ""
"Issue block device discard for clusters freed on file deletion/truncation to "
"inform underlying storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:189
msgid "native_symlink=raw|rel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:189
msgid ""
"Configure how absolute symbolic links and mount points (junctions) are "
"handled. Under \"raw\" (default), the absolute target path is returned as-is "
"without translation. Under \"rel\", it is rewritten as a relative path "
"anchored at the volume root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:196
msgid "symlink=wsl|native"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:196
msgid ""
"Configure how symbolic links are created. Under \"wsl\" (default), WSL "
"(Windows Subsystem for Linux) compatible symlinks are created. Under "
"\"native\", Windows native symbolic links are created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:202
msgid "streams_interface="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:202
msgid ""
"Select the named-stream pathname interface: \"none\" (default) treats ':' as "
"an ordinary filename character, while \"windows\" interprets the final "
"component of a path such as \"file:stream\" as a named $DATA stream. In "
"\"windows\" mode, filenames containing ':' are hidden from directory "
"listings and cannot be addressed through the pathname interface. Only the "
"file:stream form is recognized. Typed forms such as file::$DATA and "
"file:stream:$DATA are not supported. The ioctl interface is available in "
"both modes. The base file or directory must already exist before a pathname "
"stream can be created. Creating file:stream does not create file. Removing a "
"stream while it is open fails with -EBUSY. Removing the base file while a "
"stream is open detaches the stream from the namespace; NTFS has no orphan-"
"stream list, so a crash can leave that stream's data unreachable on disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:223
msgid "Named-stream ioctls"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../filesystems/ntfs.rst:225
msgid ""
"The named-stream ioctl interface is available regardless of "
"streams_interface. Open the base regular file or directory and use "
"NTFS_IOC_STREAM_READ and NTFS_IOC_STREAM_WRITE with a struct ntfs_stream "
"request. The request contains the stream name, byte offset, and transfer "
"length. Write requests create the stream when it does not exist. Writes are "
"not atomic. Error can leave partial data changes, an extended stream size, "
"or a newly created stream. bytes_returned is valid only on success. Failed "
"write does not report a completed byte count. Use NTFS_IOC_STREAM_REMOVE to "
"remove a stream and NTFS_IOC_LIST_STREAMS to enumerate streams. Stream names "
"are encoded with the mounted NLS by default. Set NTFS_STREAM_FL_UTF16 to "
"pass or receive raw UTF-16LE names. These ioctls transfer stream data "
"directly and do not return a separate stream file descriptor."
msgstr ""
